    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d701">

                <pb facs="1299" n="1281" xml:id="pg1281"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">694.001/8–2151: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Burma</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Key</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Rangoon</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-08-21T16:00:00+06:30">August 21, 1951—4
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>208. Depcirtel 138, August 15.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d701-f1"/> In conversation with Emb representative Aug 19 Assistant
                    Secretary FonOff predicted GOB wld refuse invitation San Francisco conf
                    despite USSR acceptance. He pointed out that as GOB did not intend signing treaty there was nothing to be gained by
                    sending delegates to conf. In his opinion Russian acceptance conf invitation
                    merely for purpose making trouble. Thus GOB wld
                    be accused of siding with Soviet Union if it attended conf and then refused to
                    sign. Assistant Secretary suggested possibility Asian countries wld make
                    separate treaty with Japan.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d701-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Key</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d701-f1" n="1" xml:id="d701-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d701-f2" n="2" xml:id="d701-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 216, from Rangoon, August 23, the Embassy reiterated its belief that
                    Burma would not attend the conference, and stated that Burma’s fear of mainland
                    China was the basic factor in her attitude. (694.001/8–2351)</p>
                <p>Burma conveyed its decision formally to the Department in its Embassy’s note of
                    August 27. The Burmese Government stated that its positions on reparations, the
                    status of Formosa and the Ryukyus, and the retention of foreign troops in Japan
                    would prevent its signature of the Treaty, and that therefore no useful purpose
                    would be served by its attendance at the proposed conference. (Lot 54 D 423)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
